preConditionHook:
// A BREAKOUT THAT GOES FAR ENOUGH PAST THE LEVEL TO PAY. The bar's high clears
// the highest high of the lookback bars ENDING ONE BAR AGO by at least
// marginTicks (mirrored on the lows), rather than poking one tick through it.
// Brooks's test of whether a stop entry at the level could have been scalped
// out for a profit: R02-06 four ticks above a swing high, V07-11 three ticks
// beyond a flag boundary -- the same margin test against different levels,
// default 4.
//
// THE LEVEL IS the EL twin's highest(high, lookback)[1], the merge's choice over
// a swing-pivot search: the least machinery, and the construction
// CloseAbovePriorHighestHigh already uses. The window is open-coded here as the
// Extremes loop it bottoms out in -- seeded at the window's first bar, scanning
// the rest with a strict comparison, and since only the VALUE is used the tie
// rule (an equal extreme keeps the earliest bar) cannot show. Extremes is EL
// source, so its comparisons carry the measured 2.22e-12 relational tolerance,
// hence el_gt / el_lt rather than raw > and <.
//
// Not CloseAbovePriorHighestHigh, which reads the close against that same level
// with no margin: the two disagree on every bar that pokes above and closes back
// under, which is precisely the case Brooks is separating.
//
// Distinct from HigherHighByTicks (one prior bar, no window) except at
// lookback = 1, where the window collapses to high[1] and the two coincide;
// both are authored and the default window is 20.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables entry,
// never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
// Deepest bar touched: lookback.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
priorHigh = high[1];
priorLow = low[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)lookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], priorHigh)) priorHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], priorLow)) priorLow = low[i];
}
